 ## Overview
-FineSplat is a feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) framework that predicts a set of 3D Gaussian primitives from $N$ context views to enable novel view synthesis [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the trade-off between fine-grained visual detail and the computational cost of global multi-view attention by employing a dual-grouping strategy: view-wise token grouping in Stage 1 preserves local detail, while 3D spatial grouping in Stage 2 enables cross-view interaction without global attention costs [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods like YoNoSplat and ZipSplat that use coarser 14x14 patches, FineSplat uses 4x4 patches to maintain higher resolution, achieving superior quality with fewer primitives [S1, unofficial].
+FineSplat is a feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting framework that predicts a set of 3D Gaussians from $N$ context views using a two-stage dual-grouping strategy to balance local detail preservation with global multi-view reasoning [S1, unofficial]. It replaces the iterative optimization loop of standard 3DGS with a neural network that maps input images to Gaussian parameters, building on the shared principles of feed-forward prediction [15832-feed-forward-and-generalizable-prediction.md]. FineSplat distinguishes itself by using high-resolution 4x4 patch tokens and a dual-grouping mechanism—view-wise grouping in Stage 1 and 3D spatial grouping in Stage 2—to preserve fine-grained visual information while avoiding the prohibitive costs of global multi-view attention [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-FineSplat operates in two stages. Stage 1 encodes context views into fine-grained tokens using view-wise grouping. Stage 2 decodes these tokens into an initial set of Gaussians, renders them back to compute errors, and refines the tokens via 3D spatial grouping and error feedback [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Inputs:** A set of $N$ context images $\{I_n\}_{n=1}^N$ with known or predicted camera poses (intrinsics and extrinsics).
+*   **Outputs:** A set of 3D Gaussian primitives $\Theta = \{\mu_k, s_k, r_k, \alpha_k, c_k\}_{k=1}^K$, where $K$ is the total number of Gaussians.
 
